  "question": {
    "text": "A hemispherical bowl just floats without sinking in a liquid of density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>1.2</mtext><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math>. If the outer diameter and the density of the bowl are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>kg</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></math> respectively, then the inner diameter of the bowl will be"
  },
